      "text": "To find the maximum possible area of an equilateral triangle that fits within the rectangle \\(ABCD\\) with sides 10 and 11, we need to consider the possible orientations and positions of the triangle. The key is to maximize the height of the triangle while keeping the other dimensions of the triangle within the dimensions of the rectangle.\n\nFirst, let's consider the equilateral triangle. The side length \\(s\\) of an equilateral triangle can be related to the height \\(h\\) by the formula:\n\\[\nh = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} s\n\\]\n\nThe area of an equilateral triangle is given by:\n\\[\n\\text{Area} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} s^2\n\\]\n\nWe want to maximize \\(s\\) while ensuring that the triangle fits within the rectangle. The maximum height of the triangle will depend on the shorter side of the rectangle, which is 10. So, we need to find the maximum height such that the triangle fits within the rectangle.\n\nIf we place the equilateral triangle with one of its sides on the shorter side of the rectangle, the height of the triangle should be less than or equal to 10. Therefore, we need:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} s \\leq 10\n\\]\n\\[\ns \\leq \\frac{20}{\\sqrt{3}} = \\frac{20\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to ensure that the triangle does not extend beyond the longer side of the rectangle, which is 11. So, we also need to check if the height allows the triangle to fit within the 11-unit side. If the triangle were placed such that it extends beyond the 11-unit side, its height would be even smaller, but we need to check if this is possible.\n\nLet's calculate the area of the equilateral triangle with the side length \\(s = \\frac{20\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\):\n\\[\n\\text{Area} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\left(\\frac{20\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)^2 = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\cdot \\frac{400 \\cdot 3}{9} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\cdot \\frac{1200}{9} = \\frac{300\\sqrt{3}}{9} = \\frac{100\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n\\]\n\nThus, the maximum possible area of the equilateral triangle is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{100\\sqrt{3}}{3}}\n\\]",
